WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.977 --> 00:00:05.810
MARKUS KLUTE: Hello.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.460
Welcome back to 8.701.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.933
In this lecture,
this little video,

00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:11.600
we're going to look
at reactions and how

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.000
they relate to cross-sections.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.110
So we start or
continue the discussion

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.800
of how we can relate
experimentally

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.200
measured determined properties
to the forces involved.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.910
The last lecture, we looked
at decay rates and the rates

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.530
of unstable particles.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.710
This time, we're going to look
at the reaction rates expressed

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:35.730
as cross-sections.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.840
We can start doing this by
looking at this simplified

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.080
picture.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.270
So the reaction rate
is related to the rate

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.290
of the beam, so how many
particles per second

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.830
are available for the
interactions times the number

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.920
density of the
particle in the target.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.840
So you have your target here.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.940
And clearly, the number of
reactions depend on how dense

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:13.260
your target is, the thickness--
so you go along how the thicker

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.340
the material the target is, the
more likely it is for reactions

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.330
to occur--

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.250
and then by the actual physics--

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.990
by the likelihood of
a collision to occur.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.870
And this likelihood is
called a cross-section.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.140
And we can think about
this cross-section

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.320
as a geometrical area.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.190
All right?

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.660
So let's look at this
a little bit more.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.530
We can stay with a
very classical model,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.690
a model in which we have
two billiard balls--

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.630
and light one, a small
one, with radius r1,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.150
and a larger one
with the radius r2.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:51.570
Clearly, a collision occurs
when the impact parameter

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.740
b here between those
two billiard balls

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.140
is smaller than the
sum of the radii.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.240
OK?

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.940
So now we can analyze this
reaction a little bit more

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.540
and look at angular
distributions.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.610
We find that the cross-section
differential distribution

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:13.600
is given as a function
of sine theta.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.400
We can also express this using
the azimuth or as a solid angle

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:19.960
here.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.090
As a reminder, the
solid angle theta

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:29.180
is equal to sine
theta d theta d phi.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.960
All right?

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.390
So for this specific
problem here,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:40.200
we talk about an
isotropic reaction,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:47.075
because by definition the
cross-section per solid angle

00:02:47.075 --> 00:02:51.980
is independent of theta and phi.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:56.660
The mapping between sine theta
and theta is not trivial.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.390
That's why you see this
shape of the distribution.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.490
Yeah.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:09.080
But the d sigma
d cosine theta is

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.620
[INAUDIBLE] if you look at this
as a function of cosine theta.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.120
All right.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.070
So this is just a
classical picture

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.080
of what we're going to
do later in the class.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.010
It's using quantum field
theory or Feynman rules

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.830
in order to calculate
cross-sections, or the decay

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.150
rates.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.400
But this classical
picture is really

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.950
something I would like
you to keep in mind--

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.640
the idea of the likelihood
of the collision to occur

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.310
is in units of an
area and can be

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.880
seen as a geometrical
cross-section is kind of a very

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.710
nice picture to keep in mind.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:48.300
And it also helps estimating
orders of magnitude of rates

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.680
of collisions to occur.

